{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>But if he struck him with an iron instrument and he died, he is a murderer.</b> It is a law of the Torah that one who strikes his fellow with a stone or with wood and kills him — they assess the object with which he killed him, whether it was sufficient to kill, as it is said: ״ואם באבן יד אשר ימות בה או בכלי עץ יד אשר ימות בו״ — from here they assess the object with which he struck, whether it is fit to kill, and they also assess the force of the blow according to the strength of the striker from whose hand the stone or wood issued. But an iron instrument — the Torah did not set a measure for it, nor did it mention in connection with it 'יד' (hand), nor did it use the expression 'אשר ימות בה' (whereby one may die), because iron kills with any amount whatsoever, even with a needle. And if he killed him by pushing — if he pushed him from the top of a roof and he died — therefore it is said: ״ואם בשנאה יהדפנו״; and they also assess the height of the place, that it be at least ten handbreadths, for if its height is not ten handbreadths it does not have enough to kill. And if he threw upon him a leaning wall or an object by which one dies, therefore it is said: ״או השליך עליו כל כלי״. And if he struck him with his hand or with his foot and killed him, they also assess the blow, which must be with enmity, and therefore it is said of it: ״או באיבה הכהו בידו״."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:58.888658448Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}